# Integration Routing

Spec: IntegrationRouting
Purpose:
  Ordered backend routing for ALL external integrations (channels, tools,
  MCP servers, providers). Adopted from Agent-Reach's channel model
  (research 2026-09-10; source: https://github.com/Panniantong/Agent-Reach
  — MIT — channels/base.py, doctor.py, probe.py).

Owns: runtime/integration-routing.sdd

CoreModel (per integration = "channel"):
  backends:
    definition: ORDERED candidate list — backends[0] is preferred, the
      rest are fallbacks
    rule: switching a backend = REORDERING the list (or a user override),
      NEVER rewriting integration code
  active_backend:
    definition: the backend actually serving the channel RIGHT NOW
    rule: set only by real probing (see ProbeSemantics) — command
      existence alone is NOT proof of health
    rule: null means "deliberately not probed" or "unavailable" — the
      health report MUST distinguish the two
  tier:
    values: 0 (zero-config) | 1 (free key/login) | 2 (complex setup)
    rule: health reports group by tier; tier drives activation gating
    alignment: MCP allowlist tiers ([MAL-09]) are the server instance of
      this pattern — one vocabulary across channels and servers

UserOverride:
  mechanism: config key `<channel>_backend` (env `<CHANNEL>_BACKEND`)
  rule: override MOVES the named backend to the front of the list
  rule: UNKNOWN override values are IGNORED — a stale override must
        never hide working backends (Agent-Reach lesson)
  rule: overrides are recorded in the decision trail (which channel,
        which backend, when)

SwitchingProtocol:
  trigger: active backend probe fails persistently OR upstream deprecation
  action: reorder candidates → re-probe in order → first healthy wins
  evidence: the 2026-06 yt-dlp→bili-cli switch (platform blocked a backend;
    users felt nothing) — routing changes are operational, not code changes
  rule: every switch is logged (channel, from-backend, to-backend, reason)

ProbeSemantics (from Agent-Reach probe.py — 4-way classification):
  statuses:
    missing:  command not on PATH
    broken:   exists but cannot execute — stale venv shim (FileNotFoundError
              on exec, or shell exit 126/127); prescription = reinstall hint
    timeout:  runs but misbehaves within the probe window
    error:    runs, returns non-zero
  rule: probes are SIDE-EFFECT-FREE only (version/status commands)
  rule: retries apply ONLY to transient statuses (timeout/error) —
        missing/broken never heal between retries
  rule: probe results are the ONLY source of active_backend truth

HealthReportDiscipline (from Agent-Reach doctor.py):
  rule: one misbehaving channel degrades to its own error row — NEVER
        takes the whole report down
  rule: registry singletons must not leak stale active_backend from a
        previous check into an errored result
  rule: output boundary scrubs credentials (see security DLP) — upstream
        probe output can echo configured URLs

Rules:
  [IR-01] Every external integration declares an ORDERED backends list.
  [IR-02] active_backend is set ONLY by real probing, never by existence.
  [IR-03] Backend switches are reorders, logged with reason.
  [IR-04] Unknown user overrides are ignored, never fatal.
  [IR-05] Probes are side-effect-free; retries only for transient statuses.
  [IR-06] Health reports degrade per-channel; never crash whole-report.
  [IR-07] Probe output passes credential scrub before rendering/storage.
  [IR-08] Channel tiers (0/1/2) gate activation and group health reports.
  [IR-09] A channel read failure routes to the NEXT backend (this is
          Layer2_fallback in state.sdd — integration-level instance).
  [IR-10] The integration layer SELECTS and ROUTES; reading is done by the
          agent calling the upstream tool directly — no wrapper layer
          (capability-layer philosophy, shared with Agent-Reach).

Navigation:
